  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 PM EDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nA cold front will sag southeast through northeast IN and northwest \nOH this afternoon. This front will have up to near 2000 j/kg of \nMLCAPE to work with. However, lacking deep forcing/moisture, and \nonly 15-20 kts of effective shear, should keep any convection sub-\nsevere and isolated (10-30%) along the front. Drier air then briefly \nsettles in post-frontal tonight and Sunday morning, with the \npotential for patchy fog early Sunday as winds go light/calm under a \nmainly clear sky. \n\nThe aforementioned frontal boundary will lift back northeast toward \nthe MI state line Sunday afternoon into Sunday night on the nose of \nincreasing low level west-southwest flow. This will allow very \nmuggy/unstable air to advect back into the lower Great Lakes. This \nwill set the stage for a prolonged, convectively active period \nacross the region through midweek as several low amplitude \nshortwaves send convective complexes through on the southern fringes \nof developing west-northwest flow aloft. \n\nChances for convection will enter the picture Sunday afternoon into \nearly Sunday evening with the initial theta-e surge and some \nsemblance of an incoming MCS from the west. The bulk of the area may \nsee this feature in a weakening, more elevated state, though \ncould see a few cells rooted in the surface (potentially severe)\ninto northwest IN, depending on the timing/intensity of \nconvection, and the positioning of the sfc warm front. 12z CAMs \ndid come into better agreement on another subtle impulse \ninvigorating a cluster of convection along the frontal slope \nlater Sunday night over mainly northern zones, possibly sinking \nsouth over the area through mid morning Monday. Flooding appears\nto be the primary risk, though a low end wind threat could \nemerge if a decent cold pool becomes established. \n\nForecast specifics become increasingly uncertain with time Monday \nthrough Wednesday as where the next several convective clusters \ntrack will depend on how the previous convective cycle played out, \nand how any cold pools modulate where mesoscale composite outflow \nboundaries set up for renewed diurnally driven thunderstorm \nactivity. It is worth noting that shear/instability/moisture \nprofiles will support the potential for severe thunderstorms and \nflooding, particularly late Monday afternoon into Monday night. \nOtherwise, given the low confidence, held close to NBM guidance for \nPoPs and temps in what could be a fairly active period.  \n\nModels overall continue to support some suppression by Thursday and \nFriday as troughing into the Northeast US likely forces the \nboundary and associated humidity/moisture off to the south.",
